\section{A Value Can Occur in the Type of Its Consumer}

In a dependent core, the result type of a function can mention its argument. A projection from a dependent pair can likewise have a type that mentions the pair's first component. Simon Thompson's \emph{Type Theory and Functional Programming} develops both constructions and the role of computation in typing. This chapter asks what happens after a dependent program checks: a replacement can preserve the value produced by an expression yet change the syntax on which other typing judgments depend. Simply preserving the evaluation order is not enough to preserve such judgments.

For example, a pair of type $\sum_{x:A} B(x)$ contains a value of type $A$ and a second value whose type is indexed by the first. A buffer package might hide its length $n$ while retaining a vector of precisely that length. This is useful in the IR because an access primitive can demand an index bounded by that same $n$. But the compiler must still establish that the index used and the buffer passed refer to the same length. A machine word holding a bound is not itself that justification.

\section{Worked Lowering: A Name Is Not Yet a Definition}

Paulette Koronkevich, Ramon Rakow, Amal Ahmed, and William J.\ Bowman analyze this problem in \emph{ANF preserves dependent types up to extensional equality}. Adapting their dependent projection example, let $p : \sum_{x:A} B(x)$ and $f : B(\mathsf{fst}\,p)\to R$, with $R$ independent of the argument. The term $f(\mathsf{snd}\,p)$ checks because $\mathsf{snd}\,p : B(\mathsf{fst}\,p)$. Now name the intermediate computations in administrative normal form (ANF):
\begin{verbatim}
let q = p in
let z = snd q in
f z
\end{verbatim}
If the body of a let learns only $q : \sum_{x:A} B(x)$, then $z$ has type $B(\mathsf{fst}\,q)$, not the expected $B(\mathsf{fst}\,p)$. Substituting $p$ for $q$ in the \emph{final} type of the let does not repair the already failed application inside. With a local transparent definition $q=p$ in scope, conversion justifies the application:
\[
  B(\mathsf{fst}\,q)\ \equiv\ B(\mathsf{fst}\,p).
\]
The executable code need not duplicate or reevaluate $p$; the checker uses the local definition only as typing evidence. This illustrates why conversion rules (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-141}) and IR binding rules have to be designed together.

Control flow needs a different justification. In a Boolean branch the target may know that a scrutinee $b$ equals $\mathsf{true}$, but only on the true edge. When pushing a consumer into that branch, its parameter can still mention $b$ while the available value is indexed by $\mathsf{true}$. The cited ANF work records branch-local propositional equalities for such cases, and uses extensionality in its target. A more explicit target might require a transport where that equality is used. Neither approach licenses substituting $\mathsf{true}$ throughout the function: branch assumptions cannot simply escape at a join, and the checker must account for any dependent block parameters.

\section{Preserve, Check, Then Erase}

Use a phase invariant: after each typed rewrite, the new term checks at the translated interface type under exactly the assumptions that dominate each use. Keep source and target equality policies explicit. Extensional equality can simplify proofs that a lowering preserves types but makes straightforward algorithmic checking harder. An independently checked certificate or explicit equality evidence is an alternative to granting the target an unrestricted equality rule; each has proof-production and checking costs. Markdown Dossier Chapter~29 discusses the broader idea of certified rewrite contracts.

Erasure is a further step with its own contract. Evidence certified irrelevant may be erased before code generation, but computational indices used to allocate storage, choose a representation, or dispatch cannot be erased merely because they also occur in types. The correctness argument must connect the retained layout and run-time data with the facts used to justify lowered operations. A useful regression suite includes a valid binding conversion, a branch-local equality accidentally used at a join, and a program that genuinely computes with an index. Success on the first and third and rejection of the second check different responsibilities of the pipeline.
