\section{Joining Branches by Packing the Index With the Payload}

A control-flow merge need not choose a single static index when
the consumer only needs the index to match the payload. Consider
a typed IR with dependent pairs, vectors of integers, and a
primitive \texttt{sum-exact} whose vector argument must have
the length given by its first argument. Assume each incoming
edge provides an available natural value $n$ and a typed value
$v$ with type $\mathsf{Vec}(n)$ under that edge's assumptions.
Instead of emitting separate phis and hoping their relationship
survives, build one package on each incoming edge. The join
accepts a nondependent parameter of the package type and opens
it locally for the consumer.

\begin{sudocode}
  \Input edges $E$, branch-local pairs $(n_e,v_e)$
  \Output a typed join and its edge arguments
  \State $P\gets\sum_{n:\mathsf{Nat}}\mathsf{Vec}(n)$
  \State $J\gets\Call{NewBlock}{p:P}$
  \ForAll{$e$ in $E$}
    \State \Call{CheckOnEdge}{$e,v_e:\mathsf{Vec}(n_e)$}
    \Comment{Use only the scope of this edge}
    \State $q_e\gets\Call{Pack}{P,n_e,v_e}$
    \State emit $\Call{Jump}{J,q_e}$ on $e$
  \EndFor
  \State $(n,v)\gets\Call{Open}{p}$ in $J$
  \Comment{The witness scopes over the vector type}
  \State emit return $\Call{SumExact}{n,v}$ in $J$
\end{sudocode}

For a true edge carrying $(2,[4,5])$, whose vector has
type $\mathsf{Vec}(2)$, the package includes length $2$ and
the two elements. A false edge packs $(1,[8])$. On a run
taking the true edge, opening the join argument gives the
consumer length $2$ and the matching vector; the result is
$9$. On a false run it gives length $1$ and result $8$.
The join result is an integer in both cases. No conversion
between $\mathsf{Vec}(2)$ and $\mathsf{Vec}(1)$ is
attempted or needed.

\Cref{fig:visual-148} shows why the two paths can share one join:
they pass the same package type, not vectors of the same length.

% Explanatory visual
